      picture: `The system measures what's cheap to grade, not what's worth learning. Why it stays broken: standardized-testing and assessment-publisher revenue is tied directly to compliance-metric contracts renewed district by district, giving that industry a durable stake in tests staying the primary measure of learning; meanwhile property-tax-based school funding was a specific, documented policy choice, not a neutral default, and it locks unequal pathway access into geography by design. The inefficiency amplifies itself: a well-funded district scores well on the tests that justify its funding model, while an underfunded one scores poorly on the same tests used to explain away its condition as a capability gap rather than a resourcing one; the metric ends up validating the very inequality that produced it. The types that profit from the broken state: standardized-assessment and test-prep publishers with renewing compliance contracts, and the real-estate value premium that high-scoring, well-funded school zones carry for existing homeowners. The lock-in: a zip code's school funding and its test scores reinforce each other in a loop neither side has an incentive to break. Fixing it starts with the questions below.`,
