      stoicLens: `duty to a neighbor presumes the neighbor is a known, encountered person; architecture that eliminates the possibility of ever meeting them doesn't just reduce comfort, it eliminates the precondition kathēkon requires to operate at all.`,
      picture: `The system builds the walls first and asks why no one talks to their neighbors second. Why it stays broken: the same real-estate financialization and car-dependent zoning history that shapes Freedom's spatial domain also determines whether a neighborhood has any shared, non-commercial space where cross-demographic contact could happen at all; and the financial incentives favoring private, revenue-generating land use apply regardless of the intent behind any individual zoning decision. The inefficiency amplifies itself: the less cross-demographic contact a design permits, the less political constituency exists to demand the commons back, since solidarity across group lines is exactly the thing isolation prevents from forming in the first place. The types that profit from the broken state: real-estate developers and financiers whose returns depend on maximizing revenue-generating use of land over shared commons, and car-dependent infrastructure and retail interests that benefit from routing daily life through private, transactional space. The lock-in: solidarity requires meeting someone first, and the design has made meeting the rarer event. Fixing it starts with the questions below.`,
